\hypertarget{d68d7a77}{}
\hypertarget{task-6-bayesian-estimates}{%
\subsection{Task 6: Bayesian
Estimates}\label{task-6-bayesian-estimates}}

\$ \textbackslash xi\_\{17\}=19 \textbackslash{}
\textbackslash xi\_\{18\}=69 \textbackslash{}
\textbackslash xi\_\{19\}=73.46 \$

The task involves estimating the scale parameter \(\theta\) of a Gamma
distributed variable \(X\) using a Bayesian approach, where the prior
for \(\theta\) is also specified.

Parameters

• Data Distribution (Likelihood):
\(X \sim \Gamma(\alpha_L=3, \beta_L=1/\theta)\).

o \(\alpha_L\) (Shape parameter) \(= 3\).

• Prior Distribution: \(\theta \sim \Gamma(\alpha_{pr}, \beta_{pr})\).

o \(\alpha_{pr} = \xi_{17} = 19\).

o \(\beta_{pr} = \xi_{18} = 69\).

\(\rightarrow\)
\(\theta \sim \Gamma(\alpha=\xi_{17}=19, \beta=\xi_{18}=69)\).

• Observed Sample Data: \(N = 10\) observations, with observed mean
\(\bar{x} = \xi_{19} = 73.46\).

• Sample Sum: \(\sum x_i = N \cdot \bar{x} = 10 \times 73.46 = 734.6\).

\hypertarget{347e1d32}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\CommentTok{\#import libraries}
\ImportTok{import}\NormalTok{ numpy }\ImportTok{as}\NormalTok{ np}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\hypertarget{45938cae}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\CommentTok{\# Input Parameters}
\NormalTok{N }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{10}
\NormalTok{x\_bar }\OperatorTok{=} \FloatTok{73.46}
\NormalTok{sum\_x }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ N }\OperatorTok{*}\NormalTok{ x\_bar   }\CommentTok{\# 734.6}
\NormalTok{alpha\_L }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{3}         \CommentTok{\# Shape parameter of the data distribution (Gamma)}

\CommentTok{\# Prior Parameters (Interpreted as Inverse Gamma Conjugate Parameters)}
\NormalTok{alpha\_pr }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{19}
\NormalTok{beta\_pr }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{69}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\leavevmode\vadjust pre{\hypertarget{e7f53ec7}{}}%
\textbf{Step 1:}

Find the Posterior Distribution \(\theta\):

In Bayesian statistics, the posterior distribution
\(P(\theta | \mathbf{x})\) is proportional to the Likelihood
\(\mathcal{L}(\theta | \mathbf{x})\) multiplied by the Prior
\(P(\theta)\).
\[ P(\theta | \mathbf{x}) \propto \mathcal{L}(\theta | \mathbf{x}) \cdot P(\theta) \]

Conjugate Prior Assumption:

The random variable \(X\) follows a Gamma distribution
\(\Gamma(k, \theta)\) parameterized by the scale parameter \(\theta\).
For this parameterization (estimating the scale parameter \(\theta\)),
the mathematically consistent conjugate prior is the Inverse Gamma
distribution. Since the prompt explicitly specifies a Gamma prior with
parameters \(\xi_{17}\) and \(\xi_{18}\), we proceed by mapping these
parameters to the required Inverse Gamma form:
\(IG(\alpha_{IG}, \beta_{IG})\).

A. Likelihood Function \(\mathcal{L}(\theta | \mathbf{x})\)

The PDF for a single Gamma observation \(x_i\) with shape \(\alpha_L=3\)
and scale \(\theta\) is:
\[ f(x_i | \theta) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(3) \theta^3} x_i^{3-1} e^{-x_i/\theta} \]
The joint likelihood for \(N=10\) i.i.d. observations is:
\[ \mathcal{L}(\theta | \mathbf{x}) = \prod_{i=1}^{N} f(x_i | \theta) \propto \theta^{-3N} e^{-\frac{1}{\theta} \sum x_i} \]
Substituting \(N=10\) and \(\sum x_i = 734.6\):
\[ \mathcal{L}(\theta | \mathbf{x}) \propto \mathbf{\theta^{-30} e^{-\frac{734.6}{\theta}}} \]

B. Prior Distribution \(P(\theta)\)

We use the form of the Inverse Gamma distribution
\(IG(\alpha_{pr}, \beta_{pr})\) that is conjugate to the likelihood:
\[ P(\theta) \propto \theta^{-(\alpha_{pr}+1)} e^{-\frac{\beta_{pr}}{\theta}} \]
Substituting \(\alpha_{pr}=19\) and \(\beta_{pr}=69\):
\[ P(\theta) \propto \theta^{-(19+1)} e^{-\frac{69}{\theta}} = \mathbf{\theta^{-20} e^{-\frac{69}{\theta}}} \]

C. Posterior Distribution \(P(\theta | \mathbf{x})\)

We multiply the core likelihood and core prior components:
\[ P(\theta | \mathbf{x}) \propto \left( \theta^{-30} e^{-\frac{734.6}{\theta}} \right) \left( \theta^{-20} e^{-\frac{69}{\theta}} \right) \]
Combining the exponents of \(\theta\) and the constants in the
exponential term:
\[ P(\theta | \mathbf{x}) \propto \theta^{-(30+20)} e^{-\frac{734.6 + 69}{\theta}} \]
\[ P(\theta | \mathbf{x}) \propto \theta^{-50} e^{-\frac{803.6}{\theta}} \]
This result is the kernel of an Inverse Gamma distribution
\(IG(\alpha_{post}, \beta_{post})\), where the exponents must match the
form \(\theta^{-(\alpha_{post}+1)} e^{-\beta_{post}/\theta}\).

Calculation of Posterior Parameters:

\begin{enumerate}
\item
  Shape term:
  \(-(\alpha_{post} + 1) = -50 \implies \alpha_{post} = 49\).
\item
  Scale term: \(\beta_{post} = 803.6\).
  \[ \mathbf{\text{Posterior Distribution: } \theta \sim IG(\alpha_{post}=49, \beta_{post}=803.6)} \]
\end{enumerate}

\leavevmode\vadjust pre{\hypertarget{f2856970}{}}%
\textbf{Step 2:}

Bayes Point Estimate (Square-Error Loss Function):

The Bayes point estimate associated with the square-error loss function
is the mean (or expected value) of the posterior distribution. The
expected value \(E[\theta]\) for an Inverse Gamma distribution
\(IG(\alpha, \beta)\) is:
\[ E[\theta] = \frac{\beta}{\alpha - 1} \quad \text{for } \alpha > 1 \]
Mathematical Calculation:
\[ E[\theta] = \frac{\beta_{post}}{\alpha_{post} - 1} = \frac{803.6}{49 - 1} = \frac{803.6}{48} \approx \mathbf{16.7417} \]

\hypertarget{92116b3e}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\CommentTok{\# Posterior Parameters}
\NormalTok{alpha\_post }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ alpha\_L }\OperatorTok{*}\NormalTok{ N }\OperatorTok{+}\NormalTok{ alpha\_pr}
\NormalTok{beta\_post }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ sum\_x }\OperatorTok{+}\NormalTok{ beta\_pr}
\BuiltInTok{print}\NormalTok{(}\SpecialStringTok{f"}\CharTok{\textbackslash{}n}\SpecialStringTok{Posterior Inverse Gamma Shape (alpha\_post): }\SpecialCharTok{\{}\NormalTok{alpha\_post}\SpecialCharTok{\}}\SpecialStringTok{"}\NormalTok{)}
\BuiltInTok{print}\NormalTok{(}\SpecialStringTok{f"Posterior Inverse Gamma Scale (beta\_post): }\SpecialCharTok{\{}\NormalTok{beta\_post}\SpecialCharTok{\}}\SpecialStringTok{"}\NormalTok{)}

\CommentTok{\# Bayes Estimate based on Square{-}Error Loss (Mean of Posterior)}
\NormalTok{E\_theta }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ beta\_post }\OperatorTok{/}\NormalTok{ (alpha\_post }\OperatorTok{{-}} \DecValTok{1}\NormalTok{)}

\BuiltInTok{print}\NormalTok{(}\SpecialStringTok{f"}\CharTok{\textbackslash{}n}\SpecialStringTok{{-}{-}{-} Bayes Point Estimate (Square{-}Error Loss) {-}{-}{-}"}\NormalTok{)}
\BuiltInTok{print}\NormalTok{(}\SpecialStringTok{f"E[θ] = }\SpecialCharTok{\{}\NormalTok{E\_theta}\SpecialCharTok{:.4f\}}\SpecialStringTok{"}\NormalTok{)}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\begin{verbatim}

Posterior Inverse Gamma Shape (alpha_post): 49
Posterior Inverse Gamma Scale (beta_post): 803.5999999999999

--- Bayes Point Estimate (Square-Error Loss) ---
E[θ] = 16.7417
\end{verbatim}

\leavevmode\vadjust pre{\hypertarget{0f50123e}{}}%
\textbf{Step 3:}

Bayes Point Estimate (Mode of Posterior Distribution):

The Bayes point estimate using the mode is the value of \(\theta\) that
maximizes the posterior density function.

The mode \(\text{Mode}[\theta]\) for an Inverse Gamma distribution
\(IG(\alpha, \beta)\) is:
\[ \text{Mode}[\theta] = \frac{\beta}{\alpha + 1} \quad \text{for } \alpha > 0 \]

Mathematical Calculation:
\[ \text{Mode}[\theta] = \frac{\beta_{post}}{\alpha_{post} + 1} = \frac{803.6}{49 + 1} = \frac{803.6}{50} = \mathbf{16.072} \]

\hypertarget{0444cb00}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\CommentTok{\# Bayes Estimate based on Mode of Posterior}
\NormalTok{Mode\_theta }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ beta\_post }\OperatorTok{/}\NormalTok{ (alpha\_post }\OperatorTok{+} \DecValTok{1}\NormalTok{)}

\BuiltInTok{print}\NormalTok{(}\SpecialStringTok{f"}\CharTok{\textbackslash{}n}\SpecialStringTok{{-}{-}{-} Bayes Point Estimate (Mode) {-}{-}{-}"}\NormalTok{)}
\BuiltInTok{print}\NormalTok{(}\SpecialStringTok{f"Mode[θ] = }\SpecialCharTok{\{}\NormalTok{Mode\_theta}\SpecialCharTok{:.4f\}}\SpecialStringTok{"}\NormalTok{)}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\begin{verbatim}

--- Bayes Point Estimate (Mode) ---
Mode[θ] = 16.0720
\end{verbatim}

\leavevmode\vadjust pre{\hypertarget{51fdd156}{}}%
